Record the faulting pc before the NEON spill

The panic on this port reports pc = the spill instruction itself, because that
is where a stack that has already run past its end ends up: the frames below sp
are fine, the guard page above is not, and the spill walks into it.  The abort
that this raises overwrites ELR_EL1, and the state the panic prints belongs to
the spill, so the fault that actually stopped the boot is never named.

Save pc, far, esr and cpsr after the GPRs and before the NEON spill.  x2 is
free by then (all of x2..x30 have been stored) and the dispatch code already
treats x2 as scratch.  A frame abandoned mid-spill now carries the real fault.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/exception_asm.h b/osfmk/arm64/exception_asm.h
index 6d1b1e6..e1c6638 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm64/exception_asm.h
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/exception_asm.h
@@ -158,6 +158,24 @@
 	stp		x28, fp,  [x0, SS64_X28]
 	str		lr, [x0, SS64_LR]
 
+	/*
+	 * nio: record what faulted before the NEON spill runs.
+	 *
+	 * When the stack is already past its end, the spill is what lands in the
+	 * guard page, and the abort it raises overwrites ELR_EL1/FAR_EL1 in the
+	 * frame that is being built -- so the panic names the spill and not the
+	 * fault that got us here.  Writing pc/far/esr/cpsr before the spill keeps
+	 * the real fault readable in the abandoned frame.
+	 */
+	mrs		x2, ELR_EL1
+	str		x2, [x0, SS64_PC]
+	mrs		x2, FAR_EL1
+	str		x2, [x0, SS64_FAR]
+	mrs		x2, ESR_EL1
+	str		w2, [x0, SS64_ESR]
+	mrs		x2, SPSR_EL1
+	str		w2, [x0, SS64_CPSR]
+
 	/* Save arm_neon_saved_state64 */
 	stp		q0, q1, [x0, NS64_Q0]
 	stp		q2, q3, [x0, NS64_Q2]
